 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB, partitioned into a probationary queue (3 GiB) and a protected queue (9 GiB). Before admitting a completed sheet, evict as needed from the relevant queue to stay within its cap. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Replacement
+
+Newly rendered sheets enter the **probationary queue**. Eviction pressure within probationary falls on the entry with the oldest admission time. When a second distinct request for the same key is served as a 200 (JPEG bytes delivered), the entry is promoted to the **protected queue**; a 202 pending response is not a hit and does not promote. Eviction pressure within protected falls on the least-recently-accessed entry. An entry evicted from either queue leaves the cache entirely.
+
+**Admission.** When a completed sheet would cause the probationary queue to exceed 3 GiB, evict oldest-by-admission-time entries from probationary until the queue fits within 3 GiB, then admit the new entry.
+
+**Promotion.** When a promotion would cause the protected queue to exceed 9 GiB, evict the least-recently-accessed entry from protected before completing the promotion.
+
+**Queue size split.** The 3 GiB / 9 GiB partition is the default and is configurable at worker startup. The defaults reflect that comparison sessions (typically tens to low hundreds of sheets) are the higher-value workload and that sequential scans should be absorbed by the probationary queue without displacing protected entries.
+
+**Concurrent admission.** Admission and promotion are serialized through the cache lock that already guards the deduplication map. Two completing renders that would both require eviction are processed sequentially, each triggering its own eviction pass.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q separates first-use sheets (screening scans) from reused sheets (comparison sessions), shielding the comparison working set without per-entry access counters. LRU has no scan resistance. Frequency-based aging adds counter maintenance and can retain a prior project's working set too long after editors switch focus. All three leave the external interface, queue concurrency, and disk budget unchanged. |
+| Promotion trigger | 200 hit only | Any second request (including 202) | A 202 represents a pending render, not a confirmed reuse. Promoting on 202 would mistakenly protect sheets that appear during a scan before rendering completes, undermining the scan-resistance goal. |
+| Queue size split | 3 GiB probationary / 9 GiB protected (configurable) | Equal split (6/6); fully dynamic | A 1:3 ratio absorbs typical screening scans in probationary without displacing the comparison working set. An equal split wastes protected capacity on scan debris. A fully dynamic split adds runtime complexity without a clear signal for where to draw the boundary. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Eviction policy: 2Q chosen to protect the comparison working set from sequential screening scans.
